component PrivilegedCompute {
  goal {
    Let an organization's members run Actions jobs on hardware the instance
    owns -- a GPU box, a capture rig -- without that hardware becoming
    reachable by anyone who can push a workflow.

    Gitea hands a job to a runner by matching `runs-on` against the runner's
    labels, and a runner declares its own labels. Nothing else decides. On an
    instance with open self-registration that means a machine is reachable by
    anyone who can create a repository, which is acceptable for throwaway CI
    capacity and not for a machine that sits on the internal network and holds
    model weights.
  }

  state {
    Two boolean columns, both defaulting to false so that applying this
    changes nothing until somebody deliberately sets one.

    `action_runner.privileged_compute` marks a runner as privileged. Only a
    site admin may set it.

    `team.org_privileged_compute` is the grant. A user holds it through
    membership of any team in the organization carrying the flag, so it is
    revoked by leaving that team rather than by leaving the organization.
    Only a site admin may set it, as with the runner's own mark: an
    organization owner says who is in their teams, and nobody but an
    administrator says that a team reaches hardware the instance owns.

    Neither column is declared by a migration. Both are registered models, so
    SyncAllTables adds the column at startup from the struct definition. That
    is deliberate: an entry in Gitea's version table is a line in a file
    upstream edits constantly, so it conflicts on every merge, and the number
    it claims means something different on this instance than the same number
    does upstream. The two forks of this codebase already both assigned v318,
    to different migrations.
  }

  logic {
    A job reaches a privileged runner only if the user who triggered the run
    holds the grant in the runner's owning organization. The check is a
    subquery on the dispatch condition, so an ineligible job is never
    considered rather than being considered and rejected.

    A privileged runner with no owning organization takes no job at all. The
    grant is per-organization, so such a runner has nothing to check against;
    failing closed keeps a row that arrived by a path other than the edit
    form from dispatching anyway.

    A label held by a privileged runner is reserved to privileged runners of
    the same organization. The reservation is instance-wide, because a global
    runner takes jobs from every repository and a narrower scope would leave
    the interception open.
  }

  constraints {
    Registering a runner is reserved to site admins and to organizations the
    instance has granted privileged compute. Bringing a machine onto the
    instance is offering to execute other people's jobs; with open sign-up
    that was available to anyone, at user scope or at an organization they
    could create and own. Neither reached privileged hardware, and both put a
    machine of the registrant's choosing in the path of somebody else's code
    and secrets.

    An organization counts as privileged when any of its teams carries the
    grant. That needs no column of its own: the grant is already a site
    admin's to give, so it is the same fact asked of the organization rather
    than of a person.

    Compute is metered per owner per calendar month, and nobody is limited
    until somebody is given a limit. An instance-wide default would start
    refusing work on upgrade for a deployment that never asked for a quota,
    which is the opposite of what both flags do. A granted organization is
    exempt: the grant is the instance saying this work runs on its own
    hardware, and metering what has been declared unmetered would be the
    instance disagreeing with itself.

    Accounting never fails the task it is accounting for. A job that ran and
    then reported a failure because its cost could not be written would be
    worse than a job whose cost went uncounted.

    The grant is never given to the owner team automatically, unlike the
    other organization-wide flags. Privileged runners execute on the
    instance's own hardware, so the grant is always a deliberate act.

    Every decision here is the site admin's: marking the machine, granting the
    team, permitting a registration, and setting a limit.
    Delegating the second to organization owners was the first shape of this,
    on the reading that an admin allocating hardware to an organization has
    already drawn the boundary and the owner only distributes within it. That
    is true of the boundary and false of the risk: an owner could then admit
    anyone they could add to the organization, so the admin's decision was
    "this organization" when the thing being decided is "these people".

    A non-admin's value is dropped rather than refused, on the team endpoints
    and on the team form. The checkbox is not rendered for them, so sending
    the field means constructing the request by hand; refusing the whole
    request would stop an owner renaming a team over a field their form never
    showed them. The runner endpoint refuses instead, because it carries
    nothing else and a refusal is the whole answer there.

    Dispatch is the only place this can be enforced. It is the one point at
    which a job is bound to a runner; any gate before it governs a value the
    runner or the submitter supplies, and can be routed around by committing
    a workflow and pushing.

    The reserved-label filter runs at registration and at declaration and
    again at match time. A runner that claimed a reserved label at
    registration and never declared would otherwise keep it, and a row that
    gained one by some other route would still be matched against it.
  }

  cases {
    An unprivileged runner declaring a privileged runner's label keeps every
    other label and loses that one, with a warning naming what was dropped.

    A privileged runner of the owning organization keeps the label, because
    it is the machine the work was meant for.

    A run triggered by a user without the grant is invisible to a privileged
    runner, however the labels match.
  }

  interface {
    ActionRunner.PrivilegedCompute, the mark. Team.OrgPrivilegedCompute, the
    grant. organization.OrgPrivilegedCompute(ctx, orgID, uid) answers whether
    one user holds it. actions.FilterReservedLabels(ctx, runner, labels)
    returns the labels a runner may keep and those it may not.
  }

  decisions {
    ColumnsWithoutMigrations {
      Decision: Add both columns through the registered struct definitions and
      SyncAllTables, declaring no migration and taking no version number.

      Scope: Governs how this feature reaches the schema, and is the pattern
      for any further schema this fork adds.

      Design issues addressed: Gitea's migration list is a single file upstream
      appends to on nearly every release. An entry there conflicts on every
      merge, and the version number it claims is assigned independently
      upstream -- the two existing forks of this codebase both used v318, for
      different migrations, which is how that collision is discovered.

      Consequences: The columns appear at startup rather than at a recorded
      migration step, so there is no version-table evidence that they were
      added and no down path. For two boolean columns defaulting to false that
      is an acceptable trade; for anything with data movement it would not be.
    }

    EnforceAtDispatch {
      Decision: Check the grant inside CreateTaskForRunner, as a condition on
      the query that selects a job, rather than anywhere earlier.

      Scope: Governs where the privilege boundary lives.

      Design issues addressed: Every earlier point -- the submission form, the
      runner picker, the workflow renderer -- governs a value somebody supplies
      and is bypassed by committing a workflow file and pushing it.

      Consequences: The boundary is one function with one caller, which is
      what makes it reviewable. It also means the feature cannot be tested by
      exercising the UI; it needs a job, a runner and a dispatch.
    }
  }
}
